WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:06.680
Speaker 1: I have to tell you, there are stories
we do on this show, which are extremely multifaceted

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:13.429
in terms of what they mean geopolitically,
sociologically, all these different things.

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:23.189
And sometimes there is just a story that is
too funny at an absolute basic level.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:26.300
You all know about the Big Lie at this point.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.970
I'm sure this is the lie that Joe Biden didn't
really win the 2020 election.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.640
Donald Trump really won it.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:39.000
It's something Trump and his followers have
been pushing really since even before the

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.250
2020 election, talking about mail in ballot
fraud before the election even took place.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.829
No evidence for any of it.

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:50.460
But then they really got going with this stuff
on election night, when Trump came out at

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.989
2:00 a.m. and said, We've basically won, it's
over.

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:56.969
When that wasn't the case, and of course,
a couple of days later, it was confirmed,

00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:59.500
actually, Joe Biden is the one who won.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:07.370
The claims range widely from they stole votes
digitally with hackers or who knows dead people

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:10.910
voted the mail in ballots were fraudulent.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.680
All these different ideas, no evidence for
any of it.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:20.290
Of course, over the weekend, the former president
put out another one of these unhinged statements,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.750
which now are appearing really just on his
website because he's banned on Twitter and

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.440
he's banned on Facebook and all of it.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.150
And it actually is.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:37.030
I would argue, I'm going to propose to you
this is the most unifying statement that Donald

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:41.860
Trump has ever made as a result of grammatical
error.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:43.490
There's really no other way to put it.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:48.560
Donald Trump's statement says, and I'm just
going to read it to you as written, and then

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.500
we're going to unravel the grammar of it.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.610
You really have to pick it apart, Trump says.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:02:00.720
Anybody that doesn't think there wasn't massive
election fraud in the 2020 presidential election

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.440
is either very stupid or very corrupt.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.300
This is a Trump statement I can agree with.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.630
Now, let's break it down.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.719
We you have to really take apart the grammar.

00:02:12.719 --> 00:02:23.470
OK, so Trump says anybody that doesn't think
there wasn't fraud is corrupt or stupid.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.200
So if Trump had said, Imagine this is the
way I unravel these double negatives.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.040
If Trump had said anybody that thinks there
wasn't fraud is stupid, that's really easy.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.000
You don't think there's fraud.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.640
You're stupid.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.569
When Trump
Speaker 2: says anybody

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:46.069
Speaker 1: that doesn't think there wasn't
fraud, Trump is saying, if you believe that

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:48.209
there's fraud.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.990
If you believe the big lie, that's a double
negative, you're stupid or corrupt.

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.410
And that is exactly right.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:03:00.690
It actually I was looking over the weekend,
I was saying, OK, so if you don't think it

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:04.040
wasn't, this is a statement that I agree with.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.489
Now, sure.

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:07.889
Double negative phrasing is kind of weird.

00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:10.400
But Trump is finally correct about something
here.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.860
And this is, of course, the ultimate cellphone
saved by syntax, bizarrely enough, and it's

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:18.220
surreal in some weird way.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:26.010
When you think that this guy had the nuclear
codes for four years, could this be the subconscious

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:33.590
truth that Trump actually believes that if
you've fallen for his lies, you are the stupid

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.010
or corrupt one?

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:41.819
Just Trump simply have no idea how double
negatives work, and in a sense, it's the perfect

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:45.499
unifying message if you're able to unravel
the double negative.

00:03:45.499 --> 00:03:49.870
The way we've just done and you understand
it, you agree.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.779
If you're not able to unravel the double negative,
maybe you also fell for the voter fraud, lied

00:03:54.779 --> 00:03:56.080
to begin with.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.200
And so you also agree with the statement.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.569
It's it's incredible that he has figured out
a way to really make a unifying statement

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:07.569
that just about everybody will believe and
we're supposed to believe.

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:14.040
That he genuinely earned good grades at the
University of Pennsylvania, so there it is.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:05:24.720
If he does get back on Twitter, it will be
a very, very dark day.

